# Session Operating Contract

The rules of engagement for working a MarkdownLLM domain inside an ephemeral
Perplexity session. They exist because the compute is disposable and only
published commits survive. Domain-agnostic; the targets live in `config.env`.
(Perplexity adapter, framework v{framework_version}, mechanism {bundle_hash} —
rendered from framework templates, do not hand-edit.)

## The environment

An isolated cloud container: compute reclaimable the moment the session
idles, disk session-scoped. Nothing here is durable until **published** to the
remote. Treat every commit as at-risk until its publication succeeds.

## Which controls are real in this harness

The kernel's rule is that **anchor decides enforcement**. Know which is which
before trusting any of them:

| Control | Anchor | Here |
| --- | --- | --- |
| pre-commit `validate` + `coherence` | `git-fs` | Installed by assembly. When runnable, the enumerated mechanical Errors stop the commit. |
| `mdllm publish` branch + ff guards | `git-fs` | Enforced when invoked: refuses guessed branches, never creates one, verifies the remote tip. This does not prove invocation or grant authority to invoke it. |
| post-commit `autopush` | `git-fs` | **Fails, loudly, by design.** No credentials in git config. Publication is your explicit step. |
| Tier-0 contract into context | emitted by assembly | **Ran once, at assembly.** `session-start --contract` printed the kernel + each domain's AGENTS.md into the transcript; the attestation records it. |
| domain harness hooks | `harness-session` | **Never fire here.** The repo was cloned after session start; "installed" means the file exists, not that anything runs it. Writes are NOT validated as you make them — the pre-commit floor at commit time is the first check that sees them. |
| everything the HANDOFF lists as yours | `interpretation` | **Only as real as your compliance.** Skipping it is silent — which is why the handoff prints it. |

## Credential handling — ambient, no PAT

- Perplexity has a connected GitHub credential, injected per tool call by the
  harness. `gh auth setup-git` configures the credential helper; the
  framework's git transport runs in ambient-credential mode when `GH_PAT` is
  unset, so clone, assemble, and publish all use it.
- No personal access token is pasted, written to a file, echoed, placed in a
  URL, or stored in git config. Nothing to revoke at session end — the
  credential is managed by the harness, not by this transcript.
- If the GitHub credential is absent or expired, STOP and ask the operator to
  connect it. Do not fall back to a pasted PAT — the ambient path is the whole
  point and is strictly safer.

## Publication discipline — the core rule

Publication is default-deny. The ambient credential authorizes the requested
assembly reads; it does not silently authorize future pushes. Before the first
write in each domain, read its entry contract: only a literal
`git.autopush: true` is standing publication authority. False, absent, or
malformed is off. Surface the disposable-container risk and ask the operator
whether they want to make that future-facing choice; do not make it for them.

When literal true is present, publish after each authorised commit, using the
ambient credential (GH_PAT unset):

```bash
python3 tools/mdllm.py publish --authorize-once domains/<name>
```

- `--authorize-once` is correct only after explicit operator approval for that
  specific publish — never as a post-commit habit.
- The branch is READ (recorded at assembly from each remote's own HEAD).
  Never type a branch name — not in publish, not in `checkout -b`, not in
  `merge`, not as a PR base. A guessed name can create a stray remote branch,
  report success, and make the work invisible to everyone including the next
  session.
- A branch name is per-repo, never per-session. Domains loaded together can
  differ.
- A non-zero exit is a STOP: read the message. Never `--force`, never a
  different branch name to make an error go away. A rejected publication is
  divergence, and routing it is the user's decision.
- Subagents inherit neither authority nor branch context: only after the same
  literal-true check, hand them the exact publish command.

## Commit attribution

- Author every commit as `GIT_NAME <GIT_EMAIL>` from `config.env` (assembly
  sets this per domain).
- No AI co-author trailer. "Unverified" on GitHub means unsigned, not fake —
  signing would need the user's private key, which does not belong in this
  container. Expected and accepted.

## Floor discipline

- The pre-commit floor (`validate` + `coherence`) owns its enumerated
  mechanical checks when the installed hook actually executes. Never
  `--no-verify`. If a commit is blocked, fix the cause; the message names the
  remedy.
- Two remedies mutate tracked files and must be run only after surfacing them
  to the operator and getting authorisation — never automatic: `mdllm
  domain-kernel .` (stale kernel blocks) and `mdllm session-start .`
  (session-gate re-attest).
- The install self-test's "NOT enforced" verdict is a false alarm when the
  domain has external things unverified for over 30 days: full validation
  escalates them, but the real pre-commit uses `--view index` where they are
  non-blocking Info. Verify with `python3 tools/mdllm.py validate . --view
  index` rather than trusting the self-test.
- Mechanical checks belong to the tool; your validation duty is semantic (the
  emitted kernel carries the split).

## Human-decides boundary

Some domains make determinations that belong to a human — regulated,
compliance, financial, or otherwise consequential. Prepare figures, surface
contradictions, draft records; irreversible or already-published acts are the
user's call. Record their determination attributably; do not decide for them.
